body:
So, I _think_ what was happening is that I had a system that would run `asset_server.load("...")` and then `my_assets.get(&handle)` _until_ that call would return `Some`. The assets examples in this repo show doing the `load` once and then storing the handle in a resource. I never bothered, since it worked without doing the store+load step.

Since upgrading to 0.12, that started causing issues. I've since switched to using a `Local<Option<Handle<MyAsset>>>`, and doing:

```rust
let handle = my_local.get_or_insert_with(|| asset_server.load("..."));
let Some(FooAsset(foo)) = my_assets.get(&*handle) else {
    return;
};
```

And now things are working as expected again.

For what its worth here is an example how to cause this panic:
```rs
use bevy::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .add_systems(Update, asset_added)
        .run();
}
fn setup(mut commands: Commands, asset_server: Res<AssetServer>) {
    commands.spawn(SpriteBundle {
        texture: asset_server.load("bevy.png"),
        ..Default::default()
    });
}

fn asset_added(
    asset_server: Res<AssetServer>,
    mut assets: ResMut<Assets<Image>>,
    mut asset_events: EventReader<AssetEvent<Image>>,
) {
    for event in asset_events.read() {
        if let AssetEvent::<Image>::Added { id } = event {
            assets.remove(id.clone());
            asset_server.reload("bevy.png");
        }
    }
}
```

I did some digging why this happens.   
Like the panic says, entries should always be valid. An entry becomes invalid when it is removed from the asset storage and its index is queued up to be recycled. When a new index is reserver, an old index may be recycled the next time flush is called. However when reloading an asset, it doesnt reserve a new index, it keeps using the index that was removed. This causes the index to never be actually recycled and it remains invalid. Ergo the panic.

Two things to note:  
1) When an asset is removed from an `Asstes` it will trigger a `AssetEvent::Removed` event however the asset server still reports its load state as `Loaded`. I seems weird for an asset to have been removed and to be loaded at the same time. Perhaps this should return the load state to `NotLoaded` in the asset server?

2) As described in the docs, `AssetServer::reload` should only reload assets that are in the `Loaded` state. However since the load state isnt quite correct this causes the panic. Should reloading a "removed" asset be a thing at all?
